        "content": "## SPIKES: six-step protocol for delivering bad news\nEach domain scored out of 5.\n\n## Scorecard\n| Domain | Score |\n| --- | --- |\n| S · Setting up the interview | 2/5 · Fair |\n| P · Assessing perception | 2/5 · Fair |\n| I · Obtaining invitation | 1/5 · Poor |\n| K · Giving knowledge | 3/5 · Good |\n| E · Addressing emotions | 3/5 · Good |\n| S · Strategy & summary | 4/5 · Very good |\n\n### S · Setting up the interview: 2/5 · Fair\n- **Evidence:** Learner said: \"Hi, Mr. Boyd. Uh, yeah, tell me a little bit more about that.\"\n- **Rationale:** The learner opened warmly and by name and invited the patient to expand, which is an engaged start. However, the physical setting (privacy, seating, minimizing interruptions) cannot be surfaced in this modality, and on the assessable dimension of involving significant others the learner did not proactively check who was present before disclosing. The patient later raised his wife himself. Because the setup was minimal and the news landed before any check about support present, this stays at Fair.\n- **Recommendations:** Before delivering serious news, take a moment to establish support and readiness, for example by asking whether someone is with the patient today and whether now is a good time to talk through some results. Suggested wording: \"Before I share what the scan showed, is there someone with you today, or would you like anyone to be part of this conversation?\"\n\n### P · Assessing perception: 2/5 · Fair\n- **Evidence:** Learner said: \"Hi, Mr. Boyd. Uh, yeah, tell me a little bit more about that.\"\n- **Rationale:** The learner did invite the patient to say more, which surfaced his belief that this was a disc injury from decades of lifting. But the learner never explicitly explored what the patient understood about why the scan was ordered or what he feared, and moved to disclosure quickly. The before-you-tell-ask task was only partially accomplished, so Fair rather than Good.\n- **Recommendations:** Ask an explicit perception question before disclosing, such as inquiring what the patient understood the scan was looking for and what he was most worried it might show. Suggested wording: \"Before I give you the results, can I ask what you understood we were looking for with this scan, and what you were most worried it might show?\"\n\n### I · Obtaining invitation: 1/5 · Poor\n- **Evidence:** No corresponding moment in the encounter.\n- **Rationale:** The learner did not ask how much information the patient wanted or how he preferred to receive it before disclosing the diagnosis. There was a real opportunity to do so between the warning shot and the disclosure, and it was skipped, so this step was essentially not performed.\n- **Recommendations:** After a warning shot, briefly check the patient's preference, for example asking whether he would like the full picture now or the headline first with details to follow. Suggested wording: \"Some people want every detail up front, others prefer the headline first and details later. How would you like me to give you this?\"\n\n### K · Giving knowledge: 3/5 · Good\n- **Evidence:** Learner said: \"I do have some difficult news to share with you ... The scan that we got for your back pain shows cancer, and I'm worried that, uh, this is a metastasis from a primary cancer that we have not yet been able to identify.\"\n- **Rationale:** The learner gave a genuine warning shot before disclosing and used plain language (cancer in your bone, primary cancer we have not yet identified), avoiding false reassurance and never saying nothing can be done. What holds it to Good is the absence of chunking-and-checking: the diagnosis, the uncertain primary, and the metastasis framing arrived together in one turn with no comprehension check afterward, leaving the patient to do the summarizing himself later.\n- **Recommendations:** Deliver one chunk, then pause and check, for example stating the scan shows cancer, waiting, then checking what the patient is taking from that before adding the point about the unknown primary. Suggested wording: \"The scan shows there is cancer in the bone of your back. I want to pause there for a second: what's going through your mind hearing that?\"\n\n### E · Addressing emotions: 3/5 · Good\n- **Evidence:** Learner said: \"I just wanna say I'm so sorry to be having this conversation with you, and I can only imagine how painful this is to hear.\"\n- **Rationale:** The learner offered a clear empathic, validating statement acknowledging how painful the news was to hear, which is a real empathic response. It falls short of Very good because the emotion was not explicitly named or connected to its source, and the response was quickly followed by reassurance and pivoting to plan rather than pausing to let the patient sit with the feeling before moving on. The patient's shock (the repeated Cancer. Cancer of what?) was met partly with information rather than with silence and space.\n- **Recommendations:** Name and sit with the emotion before reassuring, for example acknowledging that this is a shock and that it is not at all what he came in expecting, then pausing to let him respond. Suggested wording: \"This is a shock: it's the last thing you expected to hear when you came in about your back. Take whatever time you need.\"\n\n### S · Strategy & summary: 4/5 · Very good\n- **Evidence:** Learner said: \"if the pain gets worse or if it starts shooting up and down your legs or anything like that, or if you start to feel like you can't walk or you're losing sensation, definitely call me. Do not wait for your appointment.\"\n- **Rationale:** The learner laid out a concrete, urgent plan (oncology and spine referral within days), gave clear and specific red-flag safety-netting for both constitutional and neurological deterioration, addressed the work and lifting question practically, and supported the patient's choice to involve his wife. The patient's own accurate teach-back confirms the plan landed. It stops short of Excellent because next steps were launched without first checking the patient's readiness to discuss them, and framing of realistic hope around achievable goals was implicit rather than explicit.\n- **Recommendations:** Keep the clear safety-netting and shared involvement of family. To reach Excellent, briefly check readiness before the plan, for example asking whether it would help to talk through what happens next now or whether he needs a moment first. Suggested wording: \"Would it help to talk through what happens next now, or do you need a moment first?\"\n\n## Overall impression\n- **Strengths**: The learner delivered a real warning shot, used plain non-technical language, avoided false reassurance, and offered a genuine empathic acknowledgment. The closing was particularly strong: a concrete urgent plan, excellent specific safety-netting, sensitive support of the patient's wish to involve his wife, and a confirmed teach-back. The patient's parting thanks for telling him straight reflects a respectful encounter.\n- **Priority gaps**: Two early SPIKES steps were compressed or skipped: the patient's invitation was never sought, and his perception was only lightly explored before disclosure. The knowledge and emotion steps, while competent, moved to information and plan quickly rather than chunking the news and pausing to let emotion breathe.\n- **Top action items**\n  1. Before disclosing, ask an explicit perception question and then obtain an invitation about how much the patient wants to know.\n  2. Deliver the diagnosis in small chunks with a comprehension check after each.\n  3. Name the emotion and allow silence before pivoting to reassurance or planning.\n  4. Briefly confirm readiness before launching into the management plan.\n",
