        "content": "## NURSE: five ways to respond when emotion shows up\n\nNURSE is a set of cards a clinician plays as the moment calls for: it is not scored, and using all five is not the goal. What matters is whether the emotional moments in this encounter got a response, and what the learner actually said when they responded.\n\n## What you used\n\n| Response | Used |\n| --- | --- |\n| N · Naming | No |\n| U · Understanding | Yes |\n| R · Respecting | No: no opportunity |\n| S · Supporting | Yes |\n| E · Exploring | No |\n\n### N · Naming: Not used\n\n- **What you said:** No moment in this encounter that named the emotion.\n- **Effect:** After the diagnosis landed, the character's one-word reply Character said: \"...Cancer. Cancer of what?\" carried clear shock. The learner's response came close with Learner said: \"I'm so sorry to be having this conversation with you, and I can only imagine how painful this is to hear\", but that phrasing states the clinician's own imagining rather than naming what the patient is feeling. A tentative naming: something like Suggested wording: \"It sounds like this is a real shock to hear.\": would have reflected the emotion back to him. This was the clearest opening for Naming, and it went unnamed.\n\n### U · Understanding: Used\n\n- **What you said:** Learner said: \"I wanna tell you that we can work on this and figure out where the cancer is coming from and do a more thorough evaluation before we make any determinations about prognosis or kind of what comes next\"\n- **Effect:** This legitimized how hard the news was to hear and signaled the emotion made sense, without rushing past it. It landed as genuine acknowledgment before the learner pivoted to next steps.\n\n### R · Respecting: Not used\n\n- **What you said:** No moment in this encounter.\n- **Effect:** No clear occasion arose for praising the patient's coping or efforts around the diagnosis. Not a shortfall.\n\n### S · Supporting: Used\n\n- **What you said:** Learner said: \"this is very important to me, and I wanna be there for you as, you know, since I've been taking care of you for so long. I wanna make sure that we really see this through together\"\n- **Effect:** This was an explicit commitment to partnership and non-abandonment, tied to the long-standing relationship. It answered the patient's worry about facing his wife directly, and he responded with visible relief: Character said: \"I appreciate that. I really do.\".\n\n### E · Exploring: Not used\n\n- **What you said:** No moment in this encounter.\n- **Effect:** There were openings to explore: the disclosure that his wife did not know he was there and that he had told her it was just the back again (Character said: \"Should I have brought my wife? She doesn't know I'm here today. I told her it was just the back again.\") hinted at real distress about telling her. The learner responded with logistics and an offer to help rather than a focused question drawing out what that was like for him. A question such as Suggested wording: \"It sounds like telling Marlene is weighing on you: what's the hardest part of that for you right now?\" would have deepened that moment; instead it moved to the practical plan.\n\n## Missed moments\n\nThe sharpest missed cue was the patient's bare, shocked repetition of the word after the diagnosis: Character said: \"...Cancer. Cancer of what?\". The learner's next turn went to the plan for identifying the primary before returning to acknowledgment a turn later. The acknowledgment when it came (Learner said: \"I wanna tell you that we can work on this and figure out where the cancer is coming from and do a more thorough evaluation before we make any determinations about prognosis or kind of what comes next\") was well done, but the raw first reaction to the word cancer passed without a response to the emotion in it.\n\nThe second was the disclosure about his wife: Character said: \"Should I have brought my wife? She doesn't know I'm here today. I told her it was just the back again.\". This carried the weight of a man who has been protecting his family and now faces telling them. The learner handled the logistics warmly and committed to support, which mattered: but the feeling underneath the secret was left unexplored.\n\n## Overall impression\n\n- **Strengths**: The learner delivered difficult news clearly and, in the patient's own closing words, straight (Character said: \"Thank you. For not... for just telling me straight.\"). Understanding and Supporting were both used well: Learner said: \"I wanna tell you that we can work on this and figure out where the cancer is coming from and do a more thorough evaluation before we make any determinations about prognosis or kind of what comes next\" legitimized the emotion, and Learner said: \"this is very important to me, and I wanna be there for you as, you know, since I've been taking care of you for so long. I wanna make sure that we really see this through together\" made a concrete, relationship-grounded commitment to see this through together, which the patient audibly received.\n- **Priority gaps**: Twice, a moment thick with emotion: the shocked one-word reply, and the admission that his wife did not know: was met primarily with information and planning. The plan was sound and much of the warmth was real; what was missing was naming the feeling in the room and inviting the patient to say more about it before moving on.\n- **Top action items**\n  1. When a patient repeats a diagnosis word back in shock, respond to that reaction before the plan: a tentative naming (Suggested wording: \"It sounds like this is a real shock to hear.\") reflects it back and buys them a moment.\n  2. When a patient reveals something loaded: that his wife does not know: try one exploring question before solving the logistics.\n  3. Watch phrasing like I can only imagine how painful this is: turning it toward the patient (it sounds like this is a shock) names his emotion rather than describing yours.\n",
